<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        x:Class="AxmolHub.MainWindow"
        Title="Axmol Hub"
        Width="1280" Height="880" MinWidth="900" MinHeight="640"
        ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint="True"
        ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint="32"
        WindowDecorations="BorderOnly"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen">

  <!--
    Copilot-style shell (benchmark: GitHub Copilot Desktop), with the window's own title bar drawn by the app:
      title bar (32px) = ☰ sidebar drawer at the leading edge / page title / the review column's two controls
      (⤢ lift the pane over the window, ▯ open or shut the column) / the three caption plates (drawn by
      MainWindow.TitleBar);
      left = collapsible sidebar (brand mark + "Axmol Hub" + version + four nav items + conversation list as a
      child of the "AI 助手" item + a bottom block whose text part opens the settings/appearance popup menu,
      with the update's own button and the gear shortcut beside it), with a 5px drag grip on its right edge;
      right = content: page / 28px status strip / log body.
    The sidebar collapses to zero width; the ☰ button in the title bar is then the only way back.

    All four of the bar's right-hand controls live there because that is where the platform's own chrome used to
    be, and none of them is repeated anywhere else. The brand name is deliberately *not* written up there — it is
    in the sidebar and in the window Title (the OS's own property, which the taskbar reads); printing it a second
    time in the bar would be chrome saying "Axmol Hub" twice.
  -->

  <!-- ==================== The platform's own decorations: no title bar ====================
       Extending the client area into the decorations makes the platform draw decorations over the client area,
       and at WindowDecorations.Full that includes a title bar - which wrote the window title straight over the
       app's own bar's left end ("Axmol Hub v0.9.3" on top of the ☰ and the page title).

       Avalonia 12.1.3's rule (Window.ComputeDecorationParts) is: the TitleBar part is drawn only when
       WindowDecorations == Full; Shadow, Border and ResizeGrips come from the platform's own request. So
       BorderOnly keeps the shadow, the border and the resize grips - the parts the app does not draw itself -
       and drops exactly the one part that collided with the app's bar.

       This is what the shell self-check cannot see: a window only gets its drawn decorations once it is shown,
       and the self-check builds MainWindow without showing it. Its verification therefore has to be a real
       window screenshot (tmp/capture_printwindow.ps1). An assertion can only pin the property that selects the
       behaviour, which is what ShellCheckWindow does. -->

  <Window.Resources>
    <!-- The maximized-state glyph for the middle plate. It lives here rather than in HubIcons because, like the
         other two caption glyphs, it is authored in the 12x12 box it is drawn in and used unstretched - see the
         comment on the WindowControls stack. Code swaps the middle plate between this and the square. -->
    <!-- The middle plate's two glyphs, authored in the 12x12 box they are drawn in and used unstretched - see
         the comment on the WindowControls stack. Both are 1.25x the close plate's 7-unit square (an 8.75-unit
         square here), because a square reads smaller than an X at the same measurement. Code swaps between them
         on every window-state change, and it resolves them from *these* keys: the 0-24 grid resources in HubIcons
         are for icons scaled with Uniform, and assigning one of those here silently replaced this glyph with a
         bigger, off-centre square. -->
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="Hub.Icon.CaptionMaximize">M1.6 1.6H10.4V10.4H1.6Z</StreamGeometry>
    <StreamGeometry x:Key="Hub.Icon.CaptionRestore">M4.2 4.2H10.4V10.4H4.2Z M1.6 8.3V1.6H8.3</StreamGeometry>
  </Window.Resources>

  <Window.Styles>
    <!-- Nav items (kept from the previous shell). -->
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.nav">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="9,10" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,2" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.nav:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.nav:checked">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceSelected}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.nav Path.icon">
      <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.nav:pointerover Path.icon">
      <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.nav:checked Path.icon">
      <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.AccentBorder}" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Button.assistant-action">
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="26" />
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="26" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="5" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.assistant-action:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.assistant-action Path">
      <Setter Property="Fill" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="1.6" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeLineCap" Value="Round" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeJoin" Value="Round" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.assistant-action:pointerover Path">
      <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The bottom popup-menu entry: the brand/host text block is one quiet button. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.host">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2,4" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.host:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The settings gear at the bottom of the sidebar: a direct shortcut that lifts the settings overlay.
         It is not the update button beside it — that one appears only while there is an update to act on. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.gear">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="7" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.gear:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.gear:pointerover Path.icon">
      <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.gear Path.icon">
      <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The update's button in the bottom row: one control, two shapes, decided by class
         (`icon` = found, `pill` = ready); it is hidden while the download moves, the strip reports that. The accent plate is the same accent every
         other primary action wears (Button.send, the strip's old accent slot) — this is not a new colour,
         it is the existing one given a permanent address. The pill exists because words are the only honest
         shape for "this replaces the process"; the circle is enough while the press just fetches bytes.
         The glyph is stroked, not filled: Hub.Icon.Send is an open path, and a filled
         open path in Avalonia paints nothing — a silent way for the button to arrive invisible. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.update-action">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Accent}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="16" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextOnAccent}" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <!-- Found and ready: the circle that a red dot has something to sit on. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.update-action.icon">
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="32" />
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="32" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.update-action:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.AccentBorder}" />
    </Style>
    <!-- Ready: the pill. One word-cluster at a fixed 28 height so the row's other two controls, which are
         31-32 tall, never jump when the state changes under them. Its corners are the gear's (6), not half its
         height: the two sit in the same bottom row a few pixels apart, and a fully rounded cap beside a 6px
         plate reads as a control of another kind rather than as the one to press. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.update-action.pill">
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="28" />
      <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="28" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="11,0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.update-action Path.icon">
      <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextOnAccent}" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="1.8" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeLineCap" Value="Round" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeJoin" Value="Round" />
    </Style>

    <!-- Ghost icon buttons: the status-strip log chevron and (with Button.titlebar's tighter plate) the two
         title-bar drawers. Both sit in a fixed-height row, so without the centering they stretch to that row's
         height and the hover plate becomes a tall rectangle around a square icon. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.icon-ghost">
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="7" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.icon-ghost:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.icon-ghost Path.stroked">
      <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="1.7" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeLineCap" Value="Round" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeJoin" Value="Round" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.icon-ghost:pointerover Path.stroked">
      <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ==================== Title bar ====================
         All three of the bar's own buttons — the ☰ drawer, the ▯ drawer, and the ⤢ that lifts the review pane
         over the window — are drawn one step smaller than the log chevron they otherwise share a shape with: they
         sit in a 32px bar next to the platform's own caption buttons, and a 32px plate there reads as a fourth
         window control rather than as a drawer. 3.75px of padding around a 12px glyph keeps the plate at 19.5px,
         which is 0.75 of the 26px square the rest of the shell uses for a quiet icon button. Padding and glyph
         size are one size and cannot be changed apart: the self-check asserts the plate is 19.5 and that the two
         drawers are the same as each other. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.titlebar">
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="3.75" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="5" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.titlebar:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.titlebar Path.stroked">
      <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="1.6" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeLineCap" Value="Round" />
      <Setter Property="StrokeJoin" Value="Round" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.titlebar:pointerover Path.stroked">
      <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The three window controls are real buttons wired in code (MainWindow.InitializeTitleBar). Their styles
         live in Theme/HubStyles.axaml under "Drawn window chrome", because an internal dialog's drawn header
         wears the same plates (Views/HubDialogChrome.cs) — one definition, two wearers. -->

    <!-- Small quiet buttons in the status strip. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.strip">
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,3" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.strip:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- (The strip's accent plate is gone with the slot's action modes: the queue's line never asks for a
         press any more, and the accent that used to mark 「下载更新」/「重启完成更新」 now belongs to the
         sidebar's update button — see Button.update-action.) -->

    <!-- The queue's popover: same furniture as the assistant's context popover (it hangs off a strip slot and
         holds a list), on the popup surface rather than the alt surface, because it floats over the page. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.task-popover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.PopupSurface}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Border}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="12" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="16,14" />
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="400" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.task-title">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
      <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.task-row-title">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
      <Setter Property="TextTrimming" Value="CharacterEllipsis" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The measurement line: bytes and rate, or a percentage, or an em-dash. Tertiary ink because the row
         above already says what is being fetched. -->
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.task-row-measure">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.task-row-error">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.DangerText}" />
      <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The viewer's prev/next. Visible at rest, in tertiary ink, brightening on hover: navigation that only
         appears on hover is a dead end in a surface whose whole job is being looked at, which is the same
         exemption the approval card has and for the same reason. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.picture-nav">
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="36" />
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="36" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="18" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.PopupSurface}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Border}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.75" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.picture-nav:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderHover}" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The square caption-row button: the picture viewer's copy and close, the review column's repo refresh, and
         the settings card's ×. Quieter than the round nav arrows because they sit beside text, but still visible
         at rest for the same reason the arrows are. All four hold a 14 DIP glyph, which is why the padding below
         belongs to the class and not to one of them. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.viewer-action">
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="30" />
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="30" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="8" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <!-- Padding is stated, not left to the control theme. Fluent's Button pads itself by 12 on each side, which
           leaves a 6 DIP content box inside a 30 DIP plate: the 14 DIP glyph does not fit, and a ContentPresenter
           centres the box rather than the child that overflows it — so the × hung out to the right of the hover
           plate and only looked wrong under a pointer. Same reason Button.window-control says Padding=0. -->
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.viewer-action:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Border}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.picture-viewer-caption">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The viewer's scrim. An overlay of this window rather than a Popup on purpose: a popup is its own
         top-level window, so nothing that photographs this one would ever see it, and a viewer nobody can
         assert on is a viewer that quietly stops working. The scrim is the theme's own raised surface at
         almost full opacity rather than a literal black, so it reads in both themes. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.picture-preview-scrim">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.PopupSurface}" />
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.94" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ==================== Settings overlay ====================
         The same overlay argument as the viewer's scrim: a Popup would be its own top-level and no screenshot of
         this window would ever show the settings surface, so it is a layer of the window and asserted as one.
         Lighter than the viewer's 0.94 on purpose — what happens under this one is a question arriving while an
         answer surface is up, and a scrim you can still read through is what makes the shell yielding to it
         (see MainWindow.Settings.cs) legible instead of magical. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.settings-scrim">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.PopupSurface}" />
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.88" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The frame radius is the composer's and the viewer's number, not a fourth one: this is the same kind of
         surface — a raised panel floating on the shell, not a page section. Clipped because a scrollable detail
         pane lives inside it, and an overflowing child painting past the corner is a bug, not a style. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.settings-card">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Surface}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderStrong}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Radius.Frame}" />
      <Setter Property="ClipToBounds" Value="True" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The category rail. Derived from the sidebar's `RadioButton.nav` and deliberately stripped of it: no icon
         arms (seven text-only rows, so the nav item's Path.icon fill rules have nothing to apply to), and a
         tighter plate, because this rail is 200 pixels inside a card rather than the window's own spine. -->
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.settings-cat">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,9" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,2" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.settings-cat:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="RadioButton.settings-cat:checked">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceSelected}" />
    </Style>

    <!-- ==================== Inspector pane ====================
         The tabs read as text with an underline on the selected one rather than as a boxed TabControl: the pane
         is already a boxed column, and a second box inside it is chrome saying "navigation" a second time.
         They are drawn in the title bar rather than in the pane (see InspectorTabSlot), so they are 32 high —
         the bar's own height — and the selected underline is their bottom border, which lands exactly on the
         bar's lower edge: the streak of colour a reader follows down is the pane's own top edge. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-tab">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0,0,0,2" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2,0" />
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="32" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="13" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-tab:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-tab.selected">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Accent}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.workspace-tab">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.workspace-tab.selected, Border.workspace-tab:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-tab-select, Button.workspace-tab-close">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="4" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <!-- These buttons contain bounded, ellipsized content rather than the generic button's inline label. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-tab-select, Button.workspace-file-row">
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border Name="Frame" Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  CornerRadius="{TemplateBinding CornerRadius}" Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                  ClipToBounds="True">
            <ContentPresenter Name="PART_ContentPresenter"
                              Content="{TemplateBinding Content}" ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding ContentTemplate}"
                              Foreground="{TemplateBinding Foreground}"
                              HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch" VerticalContentAlignment="Center" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.workspace-tab.selected Button.workspace-tab-select">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-tab-select:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-tab-close">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.workspace-tab.selected Button.workspace-tab-close, Border.workspace-tab.dirty Button.workspace-tab-close, Border.workspace-tab:pointerover Button.workspace-tab-close, Border.workspace-tab:focus-within Button.workspace-tab-close">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.workspace-tab.dirty Canvas.workspace-tab-close-glyph">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-tab-close:pointerover Canvas.workspace-tab-close-glyph, Button.workspace-tab-close:focus-visible Canvas.workspace-tab-close-glyph">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-tab-close:pointerover Ellipse.workspace-tab-dirty-dot, Button.workspace-tab-close:focus-visible Ellipse.workspace-tab-dirty-dot">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-tab-close:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderSubtle}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.titlebar.selected">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-file-row">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-file-row.selected">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.workspace-entry">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,8" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-file-menu">
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,0" />
      <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="30" />
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="30" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="18" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-empty">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The empty-scope jump: a sentence that is also a button. A transparent template and no plate, because the
         line it sits on is the thing being read, and a box drawn around one clause of it is exactly the chrome
         this pane has no room for. The ink lives in the style rather than bound per control so that the hover rule
         below can outrank it — a local binding would beat the selector and the line would never brighten. -->
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-jump">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
      <Setter Property="Template">
        <ControlTemplate>
          <Border Background="Transparent" Padding="0" ClipToBounds="True">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-jump">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-jump:pointerover TextBlock.inspector-jump">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The repository tab's prose lines: the caveat, the branch head, and the mark that a path is in both
         lists. One style because they are one kind of sentence; the ink itself is bound per control, so a theme
         switch that happens while the pane is open still reaches it. -->
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-note">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11.5" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-scope">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceRaised}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="15" />
      <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="28" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,4" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-scope:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-git-action">
      <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="28" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,3" />
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11.5" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-file-menu">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-file-menu:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-file-menu:pressed">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceRaised}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-change-header">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="6,5" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-change-header:pointerover">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceHover}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Button.inspector-change-header.expanded">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceRaised}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-path">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="ComboBox.inspector-history /template/ ContentControl#SelectionPresenter">
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="7,0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="ComboBox.inspector-history /template/ Path#DropDownGlyph">
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="8" />
      <Setter Property="Height" Value="8" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,8,0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-verdict">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12" />
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="16" />
      <Setter Property="TextAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-verdict.created">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Success}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-verdict.edited">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Accent}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-verdict.unchanged">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-verdict.refused">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Danger}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-count-added">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-count-removed">
      <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="11" />
    </Style>
    <!-- The diff body: one bordered block, each line a plate. Adds and deletes carry the theme's own tinted
         surfaces rather than raw green/red, so they read in both themes without shouting. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.inspector-diff">
      <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderSubtle}" />
      <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="6" />
      <Setter Property="ClipToBounds" Value="True" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.inspector-diff-line">
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,1" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.inspector-diff-line.diff-add">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.DiffAddSurface}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.inspector-diff-line.diff-del">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.DiffDeleteSurface}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-diff-gutter">
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,10,0" />
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.82" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="TextBlock.inspector-diff-marker">
      <Setter Property="Width" Value="12" />
      <Setter Property="TextAlignment" Value="Center" />
      <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,6,0" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.inspector-diff-line.diff-hunk TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.Accent}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="Border.inspector-diff-line.diff-header TextBlock">
      <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}" />
    </Style>
  </Window.Styles>

  <!-- Four layers: the shell (title bar, sidebar, page, inspector column), above it the inspector's expanded
       self, above that the picture viewer, and on top the settings overlay. The three overlays are siblings of
       the whole shell rather than column-spanning children of it, so the inspector column coming and going
       cannot change what they cover — and they stay inside this window, which is what keeps them photographable
       by the shell self-check (a Popup would be its own top-level). Nothing in this app sets ZIndex: document
       order is the entire stacking contract, and the order of these layers is asserted, because an overlay
       placed in the wrong slot covers the shell just as well and only loses to the wrong thing. The expanded
       inspector and settings are held to the second row, so "everything but the title bar" is a fact about the
       grid rather than about its paint order — and for settings that row plus a 28-pixel bottom margin is also
       what keeps the window draggable and the status strip pressable while it is open. -->
  <!-- The window's own shadow, on the host where the app draws it (Linux: see
       MainWindow.ConfigureLinuxWindowSurface). It is a layer of its own *behind* the surface rather than a BoxShadow
       on the surface, because the surface clips its children to its rounded corners and that clip takes the shadow
       with it. The two are the same rectangle: this layer is given the surface's radius and margin, so the shadow
       follows the window's rounding instead of boxing it in. On Windows it stays an inert empty layer, because DWM
       draws the frame there. -->
  <Panel>
  <Border x:Name="WindowShadowLayer" IsHitTestVisible="False" />
  <Border x:Name="WindowSurface" Background="{DynamicResource Hub.Background}"
          CornerRadius="0" ClipToBounds="True">
  <Grid x:Name="ClientRoot" RowDefinitions="32,*">
  <!-- The shell spans both rows: its own first row *is* the title bar strip, and the row split up here exists so
       that the expanded inspector can be given everything below that strip. The 32 is the same number the bar's
       own Height and ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint carry, and the self-check asserts the measured bar
       against it — a bar that changed height without this row following it would leave a seam of shell showing
       under the pane. -->
  <Grid x:Name="ShellRoot" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="2" RowDefinitions="32,*" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">

    <!-- ==================== Title bar ====================
         The app's own title bar, reaching both window edges because the platform's chrome no longer occupies
         them (see the Window comment). It spans all three shell columns on purpose: the two drawers flank the
         whole window, not the column each one controls — the left one is the first thing on the bar and the
         right one is the last thing before the caption buttons.

         The three window controls are real buttons wired in code (MainWindow.InitializeTitleBar). They are
         positioned here rather than by a template so that the ▯ drawer can sit a fixed gap to their left: the
         caption plate's width is one number, WindowControlPlateWidth, and it is asserted against the pixels.

         Named because the check measures this strip, and because the one surface that used to be allowed to
         cover it — the expanded inspector — no longer is: the bar is what the reader presses to come back out
         of that surface. -->
    <Grid x:Name="TitleBar" Grid.Row="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
          Height="32"
          Background="{DynamicResource Hub.TitleBarBackground}">
      <!-- The whole bar drags the window: one transparent layer across it, with the controls above taking their
           own input first. PointerPressed is handled in code (WindowBase.BeginMoveDrag).
           No Cursor is set on purpose. The tempting value is SizeAll, but this bar moves the window rather than
           resizing it, and a four-way arrow there promises a resize that will never happen; the plain arrow the
           rest of the window uses is the honest one. -->
      <Border x:Name="TitleBarDragArea" Background="Transparent" />
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
        <!-- The nav zone: the drawer's button and the page's own name, in a cell as wide as the sidebar. MinWidth
             follows Sidebar.Width, the same number row one's first column is built from, so this cell's right edge
             *is* the sidebar's border — live through a grip drag, zero while the drawer is shut. That is the whole
             trick behind the hairline below: no coordinate is measured, the bar simply inherits the column's own
             width. -->
        <Grid x:Name="TitleNavZone" Grid.Column="0" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*"
              MinWidth="{Binding Width, ElementName=Sidebar}">
          <Button x:Name="SidebarToggle" Grid.Column="0" Classes="titlebar" Margin="4,0,0,0">
            <Path Classes="stroked" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Panel}"
                  Width="12" Height="12" Stretch="Uniform" />
          </Button>
          <!-- Not hit-test visible on purpose: the bar is one press surface. Avalonia hit-tests drawn geometry, so
               an ordinary label here would swallow every press that landed on the page's name — a hole in the one
               region that drags the window and answers a double-click. The conversation's title below is the
               exception, and it is one for a reason: a tooltip needs something to hover. -->
          <TextBlock x:Name="NavTitle" Grid.Column="1"
                     FontSize="13" Margin="8,0,0,0" MaxWidth="180"
                     IsHitTestVisible="False"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
          <!-- The sidebar's right border, continued up through the bar: the same brush, the same one pixel, its right
               edge flush with this cell's so it lands on the very pixel column that border paints — a 300-wide
               sidebar paints its border in [299,300), and this element does too. Hidden while the drawer is shut,
               because then there is no border to continue; SyncTitleZones owns that. -->
          <Border x:Name="TitleZoneDivider" Grid.Column="2"
                  Width="1" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                  Background="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderSubtle}"
                  IsHitTestVisible="False" IsVisible="False" />
        </Grid>
        <!-- The title owns a bounded lane: SyncInspectorTabInset reserves the part of the inspector column not
             already occupied by the right-hand action plates. The tab slot is a sibling of TitleBar, so the
             title bar's own star column cannot otherwise see it grow during a grip drag. Clip the lane as well
             as trimming its text, including when the remaining width reaches zero. -->
        <Grid x:Name="ConversationTitleZone" Grid.Column="1" ClipToBounds="True">
          <!-- Left keeps a MaxWidth-capped title pinned to the lane's start. The full title remains available
               through its tooltip; pointer presses still go to the title bar's window-drag handler. -->
          <TextBlock x:Name="ConversationTitle"
                     FontSize="13" Margin="12,0,12,0" MaxWidth="240"
                     HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
        </Grid>
        <!-- The right column's two buttons, in the order the reader meets them: lift the pane over the whole
             window, then open or shut the column. They are the pane header's own two controls, moved up here
             where they survive the surface they control — a button inside a column cannot be pressed while the
             pane covers the window, and one inside the pane cannot be pressed while the pane is shut. The
             expand one belongs to the column being open (there is nothing to lift otherwise) and the drawer to
             the page: on another page there is nothing to show or hide, and a button that opened a column
             nobody could see is the bug this bar is here to stop.

             A right-aligned action group keeps the drawer and caption plates fixed while the expand button
             appears. The tab slot reserves this group's measured width on every host, so a narrow pane cannot
             paint over the actions or intercept their clicks. -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="TitleBarRightActions" Grid.Column="2" Orientation="Horizontal">
        <Button x:Name="InspectorExpandToggle" Classes="titlebar"
                IsVisible="False" Margin="0,0,4,0">
          <Path Classes="stroked" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Expand}"
                Width="12" Height="12" Stretch="Uniform" />
        </Button>
        <Button x:Name="InspectorToggle" Classes="titlebar"
                IsVisible="False" Margin="0,0,4,0">
          <Path x:Name="InspectorToggleGlyph" Classes="stroked" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.PanelRight}"
                Width="12" Height="12" Stretch="Uniform" />
        </Button>
        <StackPanel x:Name="WindowControls" Orientation="Horizontal">
          <!-- Stretch="None" on all three, with the glyphs authored in the 12x12 box they are drawn in. The
               glyphs above live on a 0-24 grid and are scaled with Uniform; these three are not, because scaling
               a 1-unit stroke by the fit factor is what made the minimize bar render either as a dot or as two
               lines. At 1:1 both the size and the 1-DIP stroke are exactly what the geometry says. -->
          <Button x:Name="MinimizeButton" Classes="window-control" ToolTip.Tip="{DynamicResource MinimizeWindow}">
            <Path Classes="glyph" Data="M1.5 6H10.5"
                  Width="12" Height="12" Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
          <Button x:Name="MaximizeButton" Classes="window-control" ToolTip.Tip="{DynamicResource MaximizeWindow}">
            <Path x:Name="MaximizeGlyph" Classes="glyph" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.CaptionMaximize}"
                  Width="12" Height="12" Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
          <Button x:Name="CloseButton" Classes="window-control close" ToolTip.Tip="{DynamicResource CloseWindow}">
            <Path Classes="glyph" Data="M2 2L10 10 M10 2L2 10"
                  Width="12" Height="12" Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
        </StackPanel>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>
    </Grid>

    <!-- ==================== The inspector's tabs ====================
         The Plan/Changes strip, in the bar's own row and in the right-hand column: column 2 is where the pane's
         left edge is, plus the five-pixel grip the slot's margin stands for, so the strip's left edge lands on the
         pane's border and its twelve-pixel inset lands on the pane's text — no coordinate is measured, both of the
         pane's homes are derived from the column. While the pane is lifted the column has no width, so
         SetInspectorColumnVisible widens the slot to all three columns and the strip takes the drawer button's
         place at the window's left edge; the ▯ drawer, the page's name and the conversation's title stand aside
         there, because the strip is drawn where they were.

         A sibling *after* the bar rather than a child of it, so the strip paints above the bar's transparent drag
         layer instead of being fed to it. The slot keeps no background, so only the strip's own plate stops the
         window drag; every other pixel of the bar is as draggable as it was. Only the tabs come up here — the
         pane's gear, its copy plate and the changes editing trio stay in its header, which is the row a reader
         still meets once the strip is out of it. -->
    <ContentControl x:Name="InspectorTabSlot" Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left" HorizontalContentAlignment="Stretch"
                    VerticalAlignment="Top" ClipToBounds="True"
                    Margin="5,0,0,0" IsVisible="False" />

    <!-- The pane's left border, continued up through the bar: the same one-pixel line at the same x, so the column
         reads as a column from the window's top edge down instead of starting at the bar's bottom edge. The
         five-pixel margin is the same grip the slot's margin stands for, which is what lands this on the pane's own
         border pixel; it is a sibling *after* the slot so the hairline paints over the strip's plate rather than
         under it. Its visibility is the `show` the column and its grip already share — no new state. -->
    <Border x:Name="InspectorEdgeDivider" Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2"
            HorizontalAlignment="Left" Width="1" Margin="5,0,0,0"
            Background="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderSubtle}"
            IsHitTestVisible="False" IsVisible="False" />

    <!-- ==================== Sidebar ==================== -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" ColumnDefinitions="*,5">
      <Border x:Name="Sidebar" Grid.Column="0"
              Background="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceChrome}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderSubtle}"
              BorderThickness="0,0,1,0"
              Padding="14,12,12,18" Width="300"
              ClipToBounds="True">
        <DockPanel>

          <!-- Bottom: the brand/host text block opens the settings/appearance popup menu; the gear beside it
               is unchanged — an independent shortcut straight to the settings page. Between them sits the
               update's own button, and it is the update's **only** home: it appears while there is an update
               to act on and carries the red dot the gear used to carry. The dot says the same thing the
               button says, so it reads as "this press is waiting", not as "go look somewhere else" — and the
               strip's slot went back to being the queue's own line (counts, progress, failures) so the two
               never say one fact twice. -->
          <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Bottom">
            <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.Border}"
                    BorderThickness="0,1,0,0"
                    Padding="6,12,0,0">
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto">
                <Button x:Name="BottomMenuButton" Grid.Column="0" Classes="host"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <StackPanel>
                    <!-- Trimming, because the update button in the middle column takes width from this
                         `*` column when its label is long: a two-line host block that pushed past its
                         cell would paint over the button instead of shortening. -->
                    <TextBlock x:Name="BrandLine" Text="AXMOL" Foreground="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}"
                               FontSize="11" FontWeight="SemiBold" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                    <TextBlock x:Name="HostLine" Foreground="{DynamicResource Hub.TextTertiary}"
                               FontSize="11" Margin="0,5,0,0" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Button>

                <!-- The update button's host. Hidden (not merely dimmed) while there is nothing to act on,
                     so an idle sidebar keeps exactly the chrome it had. ClipToBounds stays off: the dot
                     deliberately overhangs the button's top-right corner, and the only clip that matters is
                     the sidebar's own — the overhang is nowhere near its edge. -->
                <Grid x:Name="UpdateActionHost" Grid.Column="1"
                      IsVisible="False" Margin="0,0,4,0"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center" ClipToBounds="False">
                  <Button x:Name="UpdateActionButton" Classes="update-action icon"
                          VerticalAlignment="Center">
                    <!-- One content grid for both shapes: the glyph shows in the circle states, the label
                         takes over in the pill. Swapping content rather than swapping controls keeps one
                         button (and one click seam) across all three states. -->
                    <Grid>
                      <Path x:Name="UpdateActionGlyph" Classes="icon"
                            Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Send}"
                            Width="16" Height="16" Stretch="Uniform" />
                      <TextBlock x:Name="UpdateActionText" IsVisible="False"
                                 TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" MaxWidth="150"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                    </Grid>
                  </Button>
                  <!-- The attention dot: Hub.AlertDot is the same pure red the sidebar's approval dot uses —
                       one red means "something is waiting for you" across the shell. The 1.5px ring in the
                       sidebar's own chrome colour is what keeps the dot legible where it overlaps the
                       accent-filled button underneath it. It is declared **after** the button rather than
                       lifted with a ZIndex: child order is the only stacking rule here, so the z-order check
                       in the shell suite and the pixels it cannot see agree about what decides the paint.
                       10px against the session row's 6: the same red, a size up, because this one sits on a
                       32px accent plate in the corner rather than in a row's own status column — the -3
                       margin is what keeps the overhang a corner press rather than a floating mark. -->
                  <Ellipse x:Name="UpdateActionDot" Width="10" Height="10"
                           Fill="{DynamicResource Hub.AlertDot}"
                           Stroke="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceChrome}" StrokeThickness="1.5"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Top"
                           Margin="0,-3,-3,0"
                           IsVisible="False" />
                </Grid>

                <Button x:Name="SettingsButton" Grid.Column="2" Classes="gear"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <Path Classes="icon" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Settings}"
                        Width="17" Height="17" Stretch="Uniform" />
                </Button>
              </Grid>
            </Border>
          </StackPanel>

          <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Top">
            <!-- The brand block: the app's own mark, the product name, the release-channel marker, and the
                 version line under them. The mark is the one gradient in this app (Hub.Image.Brand); the title
                 text stays flat ink so the two never read as competing marks. -->
            <StackPanel x:Name="BrandHeader" Margin="2,0,0,16" VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="7" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <Image x:Name="BrandLogo" Source="{StaticResource Hub.Image.Brand}"
                       Width="18" Height="18" Stretch="Uniform"
                       VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBlock x:Name="BrandTitle" Text="Axmol Hub" FontSize="14" FontWeight="SemiBold" />
                <Border x:Name="PreviewBadge" IsVisible="False"
                        Background="{DynamicResource Hub.PreviewBadgeBackground}"
                        BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.PreviewBadgeBorder}"
                        BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="4" Padding="5,1"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <TextBlock Text="Preview" Foreground="{DynamicResource Hub.PreviewBadgeForeground}"
                             FontSize="9" FontWeight="SemiBold" />
                </Border>
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock x:Name="BrandVersion" Foreground="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}"
                         FontSize="10" Margin="0,2,0,0" />
            </StackPanel>

            <RadioButton x:Name="NavProjects" Classes="nav" GroupName="Navigation">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                <Path Classes="icon" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Projects}"
                      Width="17" Height="17" Stretch="Uniform"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,13,0" />
                <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource Projects}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>
            </RadioButton>

            <RadioButton x:Name="NavInstalls" Classes="nav" GroupName="Navigation">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                <Path Classes="icon" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Installs}"
                      Width="17" Height="17" Stretch="Uniform"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,13,0" />
                <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource Installs}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>
            </RadioButton>

            <RadioButton x:Name="NavToolchains" Classes="nav" GroupName="Navigation">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                <Path Classes="icon" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Toolchains}"
                      Width="17" Height="17" Stretch="Uniform"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,13,0" />
                <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource Toolchains}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>
            </RadioButton>

            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto">
              <RadioButton x:Name="NavAssistant" Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
                           Classes="nav" GroupName="Navigation" Padding="9,10,64,10">
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                  <Path Classes="icon" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Assistant}"
                        Width="17" Height="17" Stretch="Uniform"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,13,0" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource Assistant}" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </StackPanel>
              </RadioButton>
              <Button x:Name="AssistantSearchButton" Grid.Column="1" Classes="assistant-action"
                      IsVisible="False" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <Path Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Search}" Width="12" Height="12" Stretch="Uniform"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </Button>
              <Button x:Name="AssistantNewButton" Grid.Column="2" Classes="assistant-action"
                      IsVisible="False" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                <Path Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Plus}" Width="14" Height="14" Stretch="Uniform" />
              </Button>
            </Grid>

          </StackPanel>

          <!-- Fill child of the DockPanel: it receives a bounded height, so the ChatSidebar's own
               ScrollViewer scrolls instead of overflowing over the bottom brand block. -->
          <ContentControl x:Name="ChatSidebarHost" Margin="8,6,0,0" />

        </DockPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- Drag grip: 5px hit area on the sidebar's right edge. -->
      <Border x:Name="ResizeGrip" Grid.Column="1"
              Background="Transparent"
              Cursor="SizeWestEast" />
    </Grid>

    <!-- ==================== Content ==================== -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" RowDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto">

      <Grid Grid.Row="0" RowDefinitions="Auto,*">
        <!-- An on-demand system permission hint takes its own row, never covering the composer. -->
        <Border x:Name="MacComputerPermissionBanner" IsVisible="False" Margin="24,14,24,0"
                Padding="14,10" CornerRadius="6" ClipToBounds="True"
                Background="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceAlt}"
                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.Border}" BorderThickness="1">
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
            <StackPanel Spacing="8">
              <TextBlock x:Name="MacComputerPermissionText" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Foreground="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" FontSize="12" />
              <WrapPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                <Button x:Name="MacComputerScreenSettings" Classes="strip" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                        Content="{DynamicResource MacComputerOpenScreenSettings}" />
                <Button x:Name="MacComputerAccessibilitySettings" Classes="strip" Margin="0,0,8,0"
                        Content="{DynamicResource MacComputerOpenAccessibilitySettings}" />
                <Button x:Name="MacComputerPermissionRecheck" Classes="strip"
                        Content="{DynamicResource MacComputerRecheck}" />
              </WrapPanel>
            </StackPanel>
            <Button x:Name="MacComputerPermissionDismiss" Grid.Column="1" Classes="icon-ghost"
                    Content="×" VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                    ToolTip.Tip="{DynamicResource CloseWindow}" />
          </Grid>
        </Border>
        <ContentControl x:Name="PageHost" Grid.Row="1" Margin="24,14,24,0" />
      </Grid>

      <!-- Slim status strip. -->
      <Grid x:Name="StatusStrip" Grid.Row="1" Height="28" Margin="18,0,18,0"
            ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto">
        <Ellipse Grid.Column="0" Width="6" Height="6"
                 Fill="{DynamicResource Hub.Success}"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <TextBlock x:Name="Status" Grid.Column="1"
                   Text="{DynamicResource Ready}"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}"
                   FontSize="11" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <!-- The task queue's one line. It stays dark unless something is moving, failed and unread, or
             waiting on a decision, because the strip's job is what needs acting on and nothing else — a
             progress bar for its own sake would be chrome. Pressing it lifts the popover that holds the
             rows, the measurements and the per-item cancel: the list does not live in the strip, only its
             count does. Its own Auto column for the same reason as the repository slot above, because a
             second sentence inside the * column would take trimming room from the operation status. -->
        <Button x:Name="TaskSlot" Grid.Column="2" Classes="strip"
                IsVisible="False" Margin="6,0,0,0" MaxWidth="220"
                VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <TextBlock x:Name="TaskSlotText" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        </Button>
        <!-- Background replies get their own slot rather than overwriting the operation status: a session
             streaming is not a Hub operation, and neither should erase the other. Hidden while nothing is
             answering, so the strip is as bare as it was. It is the only sign of a running reply outside the
             assistant page, where the conversation list — and its per-session dot — is not shown. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="ChatRuns" Grid.Column="3"
                   IsVisible="False"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}"
                   FontSize="11" Margin="8,0,0,0"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <Button x:Name="CancelButton" Content="{DynamicResource Cancel}"
                Grid.Column="4" Classes="strip"
                IsEnabled="False" Margin="6,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        <Button x:Name="LogToggle" Grid.Column="5" Classes="icon-ghost"
                Width="28" Height="28" MinWidth="0" MinHeight="0"
                Margin="0" Padding="0" VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <Path x:Name="LogToggleChevron" Classes="stroked"
                Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Chevron}"
                Width="13" Height="13" Stretch="Uniform"
                RenderTransformOrigin="50%,50%" />
        </Button>
      </Grid>

      <!-- Log body, shown by the strip chevron or auto-expanded on failure. -->
      <Border x:Name="LogBody" Grid.Row="2" IsVisible="False"
              Margin="18,6,18,10"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.Border}"
              BorderThickness="1" CornerRadius="6"
              Background="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceAlt}" Padding="12,8">
        <Grid Height="190" Margin="0,6,0,0" RowDefinitions="Auto,*">
          <!-- Named, and named with the copy key that existed before anything read it: the drawer is where a
               task row's 看日志 lands, so it has to say what it holds. -->
          <Grid Grid.Row="0" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="0,0,0,8">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" x:Name="LogTitleText" Text="{DynamicResource LogTitle}"
                       FontSize="12" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                       Foreground="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
            <Button Grid.Column="1" x:Name="LogActionsButton" Content="⋯"
                    Classes="strip" Padding="10,4" />
          </Grid>
          <!-- The band is the reading surface's token rather than the base TextBox's opaque Hub.Accent: the log
               is read beside the conversation, so selecting in it has to paint what selecting in a turn paints,
               not the platform's own blue. Its ink stays the log's ink for the same reason the chat's does — a
               translucent band with the theme's white SelectionForeground is unreadable on the light variant. -->
          <TextBox Grid.Row="1" x:Name="ActivityLog" Classes="mono"
                   SelectionBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.Selection}"
                   SelectionForegroundBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.TextPrimary}"
                   IsReadOnly="True" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                   AcceptsReturn="True" />
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <!-- The queue's list, lifted from the strip slot and pointing up at it. It is a Popup rather than a
           MenuFlyout because Avalonia 12's flyouts host menu items only, and a row here is a title, a
           measurement and a progress track. Light-dismissed like the assistant's context popover: the list
           is a reading, and clicking anywhere else is how a person stops reading it.
           The rows are built in code (MainWindow.RenderTaskRows) from the queue's own items. -->
      <Popup x:Name="TaskPopup" IsLightDismissEnabled="True">
        <Border Classes="task-popover">
          <DockPanel>
            <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Classes="task-title"
                       Text="{DynamicResource TaskListTitle}" Margin="0,0,0,10" />
            <!-- Bounded on purpose: the ScrollViewer is the fill child of the DockPanel with a ceiling, so a
                 long batch scrolls instead of pushing the popover past the window. A StackPanel handed the
                 list infinite height and the scroll would never engage. -->
            <ScrollViewer MaxHeight="280">
              <StackPanel x:Name="TaskRows" Spacing="10" ClipToBounds="True" />
            </ScrollViewer>
          </DockPanel>
        </Border>
      </Popup>

    </Grid>

    <!-- ==================== Inspector ====================
         The right-hand review pane: the plan in full, and the diff of every file this conversation changed.
         A real column rather than an in-page pane because PageHost's margins would keep it from ever reaching
         the window edge, and the right-edge pixel assertion needs something at the edge to measure. The grip
         is the sidebar's vocabulary mirrored: five pixels of hit area, hand-rolled pointer handlers, no
         GridSplitter anywhere in this app.

         It starts *below* the title bar, like the sidebar on the other side, and that is load-bearing rather
         than tidy: while this column was allowed to reach y=0 it painted its own background across the bar's
         right end, over the drawer button and all three caption plates — which left a reader holding a column
         they could only get out of through the pane's own ×. -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="2" ColumnDefinitions="5,*" IsVisible="False" x:Name="InspectorColumn">
      <Border x:Name="InspectorResizeGrip" Grid.Column="0" Background="Transparent"
              Cursor="SizeWestEast" IsVisible="False" />
      <Border x:Name="Inspector" Grid.Column="1" Width="360" ClipToBounds="True"
              Background="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceChrome}"
              BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderSubtle}" BorderThickness="1,0,0,0">
        <ContentControl x:Name="InspectorHost" />
      </Border>
    </Grid>

  </Grid>

  <!-- ==================== The inspector, expanded ====================
       The same pane the column hosts, lifted over the shell: the column is clamped to 520 so the chat column
       keeps its floor, which is the right room for a diff and the wrong room for a plan read end to end. Only the
       pane moves here, never the column's Border, so the width a person dragged to is still there when the
       surface comes back. The same background as the column's own Border, so the pane does not change colour the
       moment it is lifted: it is the same object, given room, not a different surface. Opaque rather than a
       scrim, because this is a document being read and not a modal that has interrupted something — and what it
       covers is still there underneath when it goes.

       It is laid out in the second row, so the title bar is not one of the things it covers. That is the whole
       difference between this being the *widest* reading surface and being a trap: the drawer button that got
       the reader in here is on that bar, and this is where it turns into the way back out (see the Window's own
       comment on the two buttons). The picture viewer below is deliberately not built this way — it is modal,
       its own Escape is enough, and nothing on the bar is what dismisses it. -->
  <Grid x:Name="InspectorExpandHost" Grid.Row="1" IsVisible="False">
    <Border x:Name="InspectorExpandSurface" Background="{DynamicResource Hub.SurfaceChrome}" ClipToBounds="True">
      <ContentControl x:Name="InspectorExpandContent" />
    </Border>
  </Grid>

  <!-- ==================== Picture viewer ====================
       Fills the window because a capture is only readable at the size the window allows. The margins leave a
       lane for the prev/next buttons and keep the top bar and status strip readable, so opening a picture
       never feels like losing the window. It spans both client rows — unlike the expanded inspector it is a
       modal layer, and the title bar is under it as much as the rest of the shell is. -->
  <Grid x:Name="PictureViewerHost" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="2" IsVisible="False">
    <Border x:Name="PictureViewerScrim" Classes="picture-preview-scrim" />
    <Image x:Name="PictureViewerImage" Stretch="Uniform" Margin="72,44,72,44" />
    <Button x:Name="PicturePrevButton" Classes="picture-nav" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
            VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="18,0,0,0" ToolTip.Tip="{DynamicResource PictureViewerPrevious}">
      <Path Width="14" Height="14" Stretch="Uniform" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.ChevronRight}"
            Fill="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" RenderTransform="rotate(180deg)"
            RenderTransformOrigin="50%,50%" />
    </Button>
    <Button x:Name="PictureNextButton" Classes="picture-nav" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
            VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,18,0" ToolTip.Tip="{DynamicResource PictureViewerNext}">
      <Path Width="14" Height="14" Stretch="Uniform" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.ChevronRight}"
            Fill="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" />
    </Button>
    <Grid VerticalAlignment="Bottom" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,0,14"
          ColumnDefinitions="Auto,Auto">
      <TextBlock x:Name="PictureViewerCaption" Classes="picture-viewer-caption" />
      <StackPanel x:Name="PictureViewerBar" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4"
                  Margin="14,0,0,0" />
    </Grid>
  </Grid>

  <!-- ==================== Settings overlay ====================
     Settings is a layer of this window rather than a page in PageHost and rather than its own OS window. The
     page argument against it: eight stacked cards in one scroll, where the model-provider card alone is a screen
     and a half, pushed every other group off the bottom. The window argument against it: this host is held to
     the grid's **second row with a 28-pixel bottom margin**, which is the title bar's drag strip and the status
     strip's own height — so the window stays draggable and the strip's download slot stays pressable while
     settings is open. A real modal would have frozen both, and the slot is the only place an app update can be
     acted on.

     The card is sized off the parent window: it is this host minus a fixed inset, clamped between a floor (a
     minimum-size window still gets a usable card) and a ceiling (a 5K display does not get a 2,500-pixel
     measure line). The header names the surface once — the page's own 22-pixel title went away with it, because
     a card that says 设置 under a bar that says 设置 is the same word twice. -->
  <Grid x:Name="SettingsOverlayHost" Grid.Row="1" Margin="0,0,0,28" IsVisible="False" ClipToBounds="True">
    <Border x:Name="SettingsScrim" Classes="settings-scrim" />
    <!-- The card's Width and Height are set in code from this host's own bounds (see SyncSettingsCardSize):
         alignment cannot express "fill the window, but not past a readable measure" — a centered child measures
         against infinity and collapses to its minimum, and a stretched one ignores MaxWidth by pinning to the
         cell's left edge. -->
    <Border x:Name="SettingsCard" Classes="settings-card"
            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
      <Grid RowDefinitions="56,*">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="24,0,18,0">
          <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource Settings}" FontSize="20" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          <Button x:Name="SettingsCloseButton" Grid.Column="1" Classes="viewer-action"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center" ToolTip.Tip="{DynamicResource SettingsOverlayClose}">
            <Path Width="14" Height="14" Stretch="Uniform" Data="{StaticResource Hub.Icon.Close}"
                  Fill="Transparent" Stroke="{DynamicResource Hub.TextSecondary}" StrokeThickness="1.7"
                  StrokeLineCap="Round" StrokeJoin="Round" />
          </Button>
        </Grid>
        <!-- The rule under the header, and the page under that. The Border carries the divider rather than the
             card growing a second box: the rail's own hairline continues this one down the card. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="1" BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Hub.BorderSubtle}" BorderThickness="0,1,0,0">
          <ContentControl x:Name="SettingsHost" />
        </Border>
      </Grid>
    </Border>
  </Grid>
  </Grid>
  </Border>
  <!-- Eight invisible grips along the window's edges. They exist because Linux asks for no platform decorations
       (see MainWindow.ConfigureLinuxWindowSurface), so the window manager has no frame to grab: the window's visible
       edge is the *surface's* edge, one shadow margin in, and a press there would otherwise land on the shell and do
       nothing. Each grip hands its press to Window.BeginResizeDrag, so the window manager still owns the resize —
       its size limits, its work-area rules, Escape cancelling it — and each is the margin's own width (set from
       WindowShadowMargin in code), so it can only ever cover the room the shadow has and never a pixel the shell
       draws. Hidden where the platform has a frame of its own (Windows) and while the window is maximized. -->
  <Grid x:Name="WindowResizeGrips" IsVisible="False">
    <Border x:Name="ResizeNorth" VerticalAlignment="Top" Background="Transparent" Cursor="SizeNorthSouth" />
    <Border x:Name="ResizeSouth" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Background="Transparent" Cursor="SizeNorthSouth" />
    <Border x:Name="ResizeWest" HorizontalAlignment="Left" Background="Transparent" Cursor="SizeWestEast" />
    <Border x:Name="ResizeEast" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Background="Transparent" Cursor="SizeWestEast" />
    <!-- The corners come last so that in the square where they overlap an edge, the diagonal cursor is the one that
         answers — and they are twice the margin wide, which is what makes a diagonal drag startable at the corner
         itself rather than a few pixels off it. -->
    <Border x:Name="ResizeNorthWest" HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Top"
            Background="Transparent" Cursor="TopLeftCorner" />
    <Border x:Name="ResizeNorthEast" HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Top"
            Background="Transparent" Cursor="TopRightCorner" />
    <Border x:Name="ResizeSouthWest" HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
            Background="Transparent" Cursor="BottomLeftCorner" />
    <Border x:Name="ResizeSouthEast" HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
            Background="Transparent" Cursor="BottomRightCorner" />
  </Grid>
  </Panel>
</Window>
